 65AS c  2023Wa10: {+65}As was produced in {+9}Be({+78}Kr,X)                    
 65AS2c  with E=460 MeV/nucleon {+78}Kr beam from the accelerator at the Heavy  
 65AS3c  Ion Research Facility in Lanzhou (HIRFL). Fragments were separated by  
 65AS4c  the RIBLL2 separator and injected into the experimental cooler storage 
 65AS5c  ring (CSRe). Measured time-of-flight. Deduced mass excess.             
 65AS c  2017GoZT (thesis; also 2020Gi02): {+65}As was produced in the          
 65AS2c  fragmentation of a 350 MeV/nucleon {+78}Kr beam at RIKEN. Fragments    
 65AS3c  were identified and selected by the BigRIPS and the ZDS separators, and
 65AS4c  implanted into the WAS3ABi device consisting of 3 DSSSDs, surrounded by
 65AS5c  the EURICA array of Ge detectors for |g-ray detection. Measured E(p),  
 65AS6c  I(p), implant-decay time correlations, E|g. Deduced T{-1/2}            
 65AS c  2011Tu02 (also 2011ZhZV): {+9}Be({+78}Kr,X) E=483.4 MeV/nucleon at     
 65AS2c  HIRFL. Measured time-of-flight. Deduced mass excess.                   
 65AS d  2011Tu02 supersedes the preliminary measurement (M.E.=-47428 {I530})   
 65AS2d  in Chinese Sci. Bull. 54, 4749 (2009)                                  
 65AS c  2014Ro14: {+65}As was produced in the fragmentation of 70 MeV/nucleon  
 65AS2c  {+78}Kr beam with Ni target at GANIL. Fragments were selected with the 
 65AS3c  LISE3 separator and identified by time-of-flight and energy loss.      
 65AS4c  Measured half-life of {+65}As ground-state decay by (fragment)|b,      
 65AS5c  (fragment)|g correlations using set of four Si detectors (an energy    
 65AS6c  loss |DE detector, a degrade, DSSD and Si(Li)) for particles surrounded
 65AS7c  by four HPGe Clover detectors, three EXOGAM and one mini-clover Ge     
 65AS8c  detector for |g rays.                                                  
 65AS c  2002Lo13,2002Bl17: {+65}As was produced by fragmentation of 73         
 65AS2c  MeV/nucleon {+78}Kr beam at GANIL. Measured implant-|b(t). Deduced     
 65AS3c  T{-1/2}                                                                
 65AS c  1993Wi03: {+65}As was produced by fragmentation of 75 MeV/nucleon      
 65AS2c  {+78}Kr beam by an enriched {+58}Ni target and mass separation at NSCL.
 65AS3c  Measured T{-1/2}.                                                      
 65AS c  1991Mo10: {+65}As was produced by fragmentation of 65 MeV/nucleon      
 65AS2c  {+78}Kr beam on an enriched {+58}Ni target at NSCL. First observation  
 65AS3c  of {+65}As as recognized in 2010Sh34 compilation of isotope discovery  
 65AS c  1991XuZZ: {+65}As was produced by fragmentation of 20 MeV/nucleon      
 65AS2c  {+64}Zn beam incident on a Be target and mass separation at RIKEN;     
 65AS3c  claims to be first identification of {+65}As; no other data.           
 65AS c  1990Ro15: search for proton radioactivity of {+65}As was made with     
 65AS2c  {+40}Ca({+32}S,|ap2n) at E({+32}S)=200 MeV and {+40}Ca({+28}Si,p2n) at 
 65AS3c  E({+28}Si)=175 MeV and mass separation at LBNL. No such activity was   
 65AS4c  observed in the proton energy range of 200-700 keV. Authors conclude   
 65AS5c  that {+65}As must decay mostly by positron emission or by proton       
 65AS6c  emission with T{-1/2} < 100 |ms.                                       
 65AS c  1989Ho19: search for proton radioactivity of {+65}As was made with     
 65AS2c  {+40}Ca({+28}Si,p2n) at E({+28}Si)=87 MeV and mass separation at Orsay.
 65AS3c  They looked for proton radioactivity in the energy range 250-600 keV   
 65AS4c  and half-life of 10 |ms to 100 ms. No such activity was observed and   
 65AS5c  the authors established a lower production cross section limit of      
 65AS6c  1|*10{+-6} barn.                                                       
 65AS c  1988HoZL, 1989HoZQ: search for proton radioactivity of {+65}As was made
 65AS2c  with {+40}Ca({+28}Si,p2n) at E({+28}Si)=140 MeV and separation of      
 65AS3c  reaction products with a mass analyzer at Daresbury. No evidence was   
 65AS4c  found for proton decay of {+65}As; the authors conclude that {+65}As   
 65AS5c  decays predominantly by positron emission with a weak proton decay     
 65AS6c  branch or its half-life is less than 5 |ms which is below the detection
 65AS7c  capability of their equipment.                                         
